# Codebase context via Scout

**Always run `scout context` before reading any files in a repo.** This gives you the full repo structure and the user's active working set in ~700 tokens instead of opening files blind. Only read full files when you need to edit them.

## Commands

- `scout context` - L0 workset (active changes) + L1 repo map (directory summaries). Start here, every time.
- `scout context --full` - also include L2 file cards for related files. Use when you need more detail.
- `scout card <file>` - compact summary of a specific file. Run this before reading a file in full.
- `scout tree` - directory-level overview of the entire repo.
- `scout ls <dir>` - list all file cards in a directory.

## Setup

If `scout context` fails with a "not initialized" error, run `scout init . && scout warm` first, then retry.

## Key rule

Do not open files just to understand what they contain. Use scout cards instead. Only read the full file when you need to edit it or see implementation details.
